</span><span id="id2" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_correspondence ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span>Corresponding author.</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Deep neural networks (DNNs) have significantly advanced machine learning, with model depth playing a central role in their successes.
The dynamical system modeling approach has recently emerged as a powerful framework, offering new mathematical insights into the structure and learning behavior of DNNs.
In this work, we establish generalization error bounds for both discrete- and continuous-time residual networks (ResNets) by combining Rademacher complexity, flow maps of dynamical systems, and the convergence behavior of ResNets in the deep-layer limit.
The resulting bounds are of order <math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(1/\sqrt{S})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mi>S</mi></msqrt></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(1/\sqrt{S})</annotation></semantics></math> with respect to the number of training samples <math id="abstract1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>, and include a structure-dependent negative term, yielding depth-uniform and asymptotic generalization bounds under milder assumptions.
These findings provide a unified understanding of generalization across both discrete- and continuous-time ResNets, helping to close the gap in both the order of sample complexity and assumptions between the discrete- and continuous-time settings.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_classification"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_classification">keywords</h6>
Deep learning, dynamical system, generalization bound, Rademacher complexity, asymptotic behavior

</div>
<div id="acknowledgements1" class="ltx_acknowledgements">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_acknowledgements">Funding.</h6>XXX
</div><span id="id3" class="ltx_note ltx_role_runningheads"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">runningheads: </span>Generalization Behavior of Deep Residual Networks / Jinshu Huang, Mingfei Sun, Chunlin Wu</span></span></span>
<div class="ltx_classification"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_classification">MSC</h6>
68T07, 68Q87, 68V15, 37N99

</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Deep learning has achieved remarkable successes across diverse applications in recent years. A key driver behind these successes is the increasing depth of neural networks, which enables the hierarchical extraction of complex features and representations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>.
Despite these empirical advances, the fundamental properties of deep neural networks (DNNs) remain not fully understood due to their inherent complexity.
The expressivity and approximation, generalization, and optimization constitute the essential components of the mathematical mechanisms underlying deep learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>.
Among them, understanding generalization behavior is one of the most important aspects, as it directly relates to the ability of DNNs to perform well on unseen data beyond the training set with a sample size <math id="S1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The generalization theory serves as a fundamental method for characterizing the predictive performance of DNNs on unseen data. Early works, such as <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>, proposed upper bounds on the Vapnik-Chervonenkis (VC) dimension for certain classes of DNNs, which are related to generalization theory.
Later, Neyshabur et al.<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite> derived a generalization bound of order <math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(1/\sqrt{S})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mi>S</mi></msqrt></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(1/\sqrt{S})</annotation></semantics></math> based on Rademacher complexity, with a coefficient exponentially depending on network depth in the case of ReLU networks.
Bartlett et al.<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite> introduced a spectral normalized margin-based bound for DNNs on the classification task, still of order <math id="S1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(1/\sqrt{S})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mi>S</mi></msqrt></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(1/\sqrt{S})</annotation></semantics></math> and with only polynomial dependence on network depth. A similar result was obtained by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite> in the probably approximately correct (PAC) Bayesian framework.
Further studies have investigated the generalization behavior of ResNets <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>.
Li et al.<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite> derived tighter margin-based bounds for general architectures, including CNNs and ResNets, explicitly capturing the effects of depth, width, and Lipschitz continuity.
He et al.<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite> investigated the influence of residual connections on the hypothesis complexity via covering numbers, showing that, when the total number of layers is fixed, residual connections do not increase the hypothesis complexity compared to chain-like networks.
These results all yield generalization bounds of order <math id="S1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(1/\sqrt{S})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mi>S</mi></msqrt></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(1/\sqrt{S})</annotation></semantics></math>, while the constants involved may still grow rapidly with architectural parameters like depth unless additional structural or norm-based constraints are imposed.
From a different perspective, E, Ma, and Wang <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite> studied the Rademacher complexity of residual networks under a weighted path norm constraint, and derived generalization bounds of order <math id="S1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(1/\sqrt{S})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mi>S</mi></msqrt></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(1/\sqrt{S})</annotation></semantics></math> without explicit dependence on the network depth. Their analysis highlights the role of norm-based capacity control in mitigating the adverse effects of depth and width, and provides insight into why deep and wide ResNets can still generalize well in practice.
Some other research investigates the impact of training algorithms on generalization performance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>, <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>]</cite>, often leveraging algorithmic stability or robustness to derive generalization bounds, which typically do not improve upon the standard rate of order <math id="S1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(1/\sqrt{S})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mi>S</mi></msqrt></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(1/\sqrt{S})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">From the viewpoint of neural ordinary differential equations (ODEs) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>, Marion <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite> used a covering number reasoning approach to establish generalization bounds for a class of continuous-time models with time-varying weights under the assumption of Lipschitz continuity in time. The resulting bounds given in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite> are of sample dependence of order <math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(1/\sqrt[4]{S})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mroot><mi>S</mi><mn>4</mn></mroot></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(1/\sqrt[4]{S})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Closely related to Marion <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite>, Bleistein and Guilloux <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite> studied the generalization properties of Neural Controlled Differential Equations (NCDEs), which model supervised learning from irregularly sampled time series via controlled differential equations. Using continuity properties of CDE flows and Lipschitz-based complexity arguments similar to those in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite>, they derived generalization bounds of order
<math id="S1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(1/\sqrt[4]{S})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mroot><mi>S</mi><mn>4</mn></mroot></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(1/\sqrt[4]{S})</annotation></semantics></math> under suitable assumptions. More recently, Hanson and Raginsky <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite> analyzed the generalization properties of neural ODEs through a Chen–Fliess series expansion, which represents continuous-depth models as infinitely wide, single-layer networks whose features are given by iterated Lie derivatives. This reformulation enables a compact analysis of the Rademacher complexity of neural ODEs and leads to generalization bounds of order <math id="S1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(1/\sqrt{S})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mi>S</mi></msqrt></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(1/\sqrt{S})</annotation></semantics></math>. While this approach provides sharp sample complexity guarantees for continuous-depth models, it does not address the relationship between discrete-time ResNets and their continuous-depth limits, nor does it study the consistency of generalization behavior with respect to network depth.
We note that, despite these recent advances for continuous-depth models, existing analyses do not yet provide a unified and depth-consistent generalization theory that simultaneously covers discrete-time ResNets and their continuous-time limits with matching <math id="S1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(1/\sqrt{S})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mi>S</mi></msqrt></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(1/\sqrt{S})</annotation></semantics></math> sample dependence.
<span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">In this work, we aim to study the consistency of generalization behavior of ResNets in the deep-layer limit, and establish depth-uniform generalization error bounds that are compatible for both discrete-time ResNets and their continuous-time limit models.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Recently, a dynamical system modeling approach for DNNs has been proposed, offering a new mathematical explanation of their structural and learning properties, and enabling asymptotic analysis as the number of layers tends to infinity.
The pioneering works <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite> revealed that ResNets <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite> can be interpreted as discrete-time approximations of ODEs. This interpretation has inspired subsequent research yielding both theoretical insights <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>, <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>, <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite> and practical innovations like architecture design <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>, <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>, <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite> and training algorithms <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>, <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>.
For instances, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite> investigates continuous-time ResNets and establishes general sufficient conditions for their universal approximation capabilities in <math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{p}</annotation></semantics></math> spaces from the approximation perspective, while Li et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite> studied the approximation and optimization theory of linear continuous-time recurrent neural networks (RNNs), providing a rigorous characterization of the relationship between memory structures and learning efficiency in linear dynamical models.
Furthermore, the dynamical system perspective enables the analysis of the asymptotic behavior of deep networks by establishing the convergence of discrete-time learning problems to their continuous-time counterparts <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>, thereby providing a theoretical foundation for understanding the stability and convergence of the learning problems of DNNs in the deep-layer limit. In this context, the term “dynamical system framework” refers to a unified modeling and analytical viewpoint that encompasses both discrete-time ResNets and their continuous-time limits.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">In this paper, we study the generalization behavior of ResNets within the dynamical system modeling framework <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>.</span>
In contrast to approaches that start directly from continuous-time neural ODEs, our analysis proceeds from discrete ResNets and exploits their convergence to continuous-time dynamics.
This enables us to examine the asymptotic behavior of generalization error with growing network depth, revealing a novel link between architectural stability and generalization in the deep-layer limit.
Our contributions are summarized as follows:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.ix1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(i)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Within a dynamical system modeling framework, we establish generalization error bounds simultaneously for both discrete-time ResNets and their continuous-time counterparts.
The resulting bounds are of optimal order <math id="S1.I1.ix1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(1/\sqrt{S})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mi>S</mi></msqrt></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(1/\sqrt{S})</annotation></semantics></math> in both settings and are compatible across the discrete-to-continuous transition.
In particular, they improve upon existing results that treat discrete- and continuous-time models jointly, such as <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite>, by providing sharper sample-complexity dependence.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.ix2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(ii)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our bounds are derived via a new contraction-type inequality for a broad class of activation functions, capturing structural properties beyond standard Lipschitz continuity.
This leads to a refined bound structure consisting of a depth-independent leading term together with a non-positive, depth-dependent structural correction term induced by the activation functions.
As a consequence, the generalization error admits an explicit depth-uniform upper bound.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S1.p5.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p5.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">These results imply that increasing network depth does not lead to an accumulation of generalization error, which is consistent with empirical observations of ResNets in practical applications.
In contrast to prior works on discrete-time ResNets <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>, <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>, our analysis further admits an asymptotic interpretation as the network depth tends to infinity, suggesting that the generalization capacity of deep ResNets can remain stable in the deep-layer limit.
Moreover, compared to continuous-time analyses based on the neural ODE perspective <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>, our approach is grounded in a dynamical system framework that treats discrete- and continuous-time ResNets in a unified manner.
</span>
We also validate our theoretical results through experiments on CIFAR10 and CIFAR100 image classification tasks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">The remainder of this paper is organized as follows.
Section <a href="#S2" title="2 Notations and problem formulation ‣ On the Generalization Behavior of Deep Residual Networks From a Dynamical System Perspective" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> formulates the generalization problem for discrete- and continuous-time ResNets. In Section <a href="#S3" title="3 Main results and application ‣ On the Generalization Behavior of Deep Residual Networks From a Dynamical System Perspective" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, we present our main theoretical results, including generalization bounds for discrete- and continuous-time ResNets. Section <a href="#S4" title="4 Proofs ‣ On the Generalization Behavior of Deep Residual Networks From a Dynamical System Perspective" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> provides detailed proofs. In Section <a href="#S5" title="5 Experiments ‣ On the Generalization Behavior of Deep Residual Networks From a Dynamical System Perspective" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, we present some simple numerical experiments to validate the results. Finally, Section <a href="#S6" title="6 Conclusion ‣ On the Generalization Behavior of Deep Residual Networks From a Dynamical System Perspective" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> concludes the paper.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Notations and problem formulation</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we give some notations and introduce the learning problems for ResNets from the dynamical system viewpoint. Then we describe the generalization problem in neural network supervised learning.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Notations</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Throughout this paper, we adopt the following notations. Let <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℝ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the set of natural and real numbers, respectively.
Scalars are denoted with italic letters (e.g., <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\in\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\in\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math>), vectors and matrices are denoted with lowercase and capital in straight letters (e.g., <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{c}\in\mathbb{R}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{c}\in\mathbb{R}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{W}\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{W}\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times N}</annotation></semantics></math>). Vector- and matrix-valued functions of time variable <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> are written in boldface, like <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{c}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝕔</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{c}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{W}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝕎</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{W}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math>.
For a Lipschitz continuous function <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>, its Lipschitz constant is denoted by <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Lip}_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Lip</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Lip}_{f}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th element of vector <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{c}</annotation></semantics></math> is written as <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{c}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{c}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th row of matrix <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{C}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m16" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathrm{C}_{i,:}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mo>:</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{C}_{i,:}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>l</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>l</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> norms for vectors or matrices in Euclidean space are denoted as <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m19" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\|\cdot\|_{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∥</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em">∥</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\cdot\|_{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m20" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\|\cdot\|_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∥</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em">∥</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\cdot\|_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively.
Let <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> be a vector space. The notation <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>N</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math> repents the Cartesian product space of <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underbrace{\mathcal{X}\times\ldots\times\mathcal{X}}_{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mi>N</mi></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underbrace{\mathcal{X}\times\ldots\times\mathcal{X}}_{N}</annotation></semantics></math>. The characteristic function of the set <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math> is denoted as <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{1}_{\Lambda}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{1}_{\Lambda}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math>. The space of functions that are continuous on <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega\subset\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega\subset\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> is denoted by <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}(\Omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}(\Omega)</annotation></semantics></math>.
The Sobolev space of functions that are <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>-times weakly differentiable and each weak derivative is <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> integrable on <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega</annotation></semantics></math> is denoted by <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}^{q}(\Omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}^{q}(\Omega)</annotation></semantics></math>. For the reader’s convenience, the key notations used throughout the paper are summarized in Table <a href="#S2.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 2.1 Notations ‣ 2 Notations and problem formulation ‣ On the Generalization Behavior of Deep Residual Networks From a Dynamical System Perspective" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.T1" class="ltx_table">
